## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a scalable, auditable access model that goes beyond basic cross‑account roles, incorporating Just‑In‑Time elevation, ephemeral SAP credentials, and automated rotation/revocation flows. Also checks integration with Secrets Manager, Step Functions, and policy boundaries (SCPs, CloudTrail, Config).

## Key Concepts

- Just‑In‑Time elevation across accounts
- Ephemeral SAP credentials via Secrets Manager
- Cross‑account IAM trust and least privilege
- SCPs and policy boundaries
- Auditing with CloudTrail/Config
- Automated rotation with Step Functions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal IaC snippet (conceptual) to create cross‑account trust role and a Step Functions workflow
const role = new iam.Role(this, 'SAPCrossAccountRole', {
  assumedBy: new iam.AccountPrincipal('ACCOUNT_PROD'),
  description: 'Cross‑account SAP operator role for JIT elevation'
});
// Attach least‑privilege policy and trust from other accounts as needed

const statemachine = new sfn.StateMachine(this, 'SAPCredentialRotation', {
  definition: /* steps: request → authorize → issue‑cred → rotate‑secret → revoke */ new sfn.Pass(this, 'Start')
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate revocation latency and policy drift?
- How would SAP user lifecycle changes propagate to IAM role mappings?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of cross‑account IAM with MFA, least privilege, and IaC integration in a real SAP on AWS scenario.

## Key Concepts

- STS assumeRole with MFA
- IAM trust policies and cross‑account access
- Least privilege for SAP HANA actions
- Terraform for IAM role and trust policy
- CloudTrail auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform: IAM role with MFA‑enforced trust
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "sap-operator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:root" }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Condition = { aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = "true" }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
// Terraform: least-privilege SAP actions
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-policy"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = ["sap:Read", "sap:Execute"]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
# CLI test: assume role with MFA and perform a harmless SAP action
aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/sap_operator --role-session-name sap-test \
  --serial-number arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:mfa/user --token-code 123456
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ongoing policy least-privilege using AWS Config or IAM Access Analyzer?
- What changes would you make to rotate credentials or rotate the operator role across regions?
